From 59acb8fc0ff6ae7c72b4fe21239dabbc2f88ec24 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 14:26:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(cron): a run cut by the owner's restart is rerun
 once, never booked failed

An instant hub core update cut agent cron runs and booked them failed with no rerun
(t_01a1d009633f359e, three live samples on hermes-hub):

1. direct `cron run` in flight: the owner's drain cleared the claim, the run failed
   "cron fire ownership was lost", and restart_rerun skipped it because an admission
   journal existed ("its admission journal owns recovery") - but journal recovery only
   books results, it never reruns.
2. a fire submitted while the owner drained: refused runtime_draining, then booked
   "Cron admission missing" (or paused on an unverified admission).
3. a builtin fire on a restart-safe worker: the worker lost the owner, the next owner's
   boot settled the lost turn `interrupted`, and journal recovery booked "failed -
   interrupted", streak +1, next run a year out.

Now:
- The owner (gateway/session_cron.py) flags `_restart_cut` on a failed cron result when
  its own drain stopped the run (interrupt phase, or a non-operator cancel while
  draining), and on a status read of an admission lost across the restart (terminal
  `interrupted` with no result). A run that finished anyway keeps its verdict.
- Whoever books that result - the firer's tail or the next gateway's journal recovery -
  settles it via cron/restart_cut.py: shutdown-interrupted bookkeeping (no streak tick,
  no failure notice, suppressed delivery), the uncertainty pause lifted, and one rerun
  through restart_rerun (extra fire that restores the far-future slot; one-shots
  re-armed). A rerun cut again is not rerun a second time.
- A manual fire the owner refused while draining is deferred to the next gateway the
  same way; the refusal is journaled first so recovery can defer it too when the
  absence check was lost with the owner.

Tests: tests/cron/test_restart_cut.py (8 RED on origin/main, 12 GREEN).
